    {"id": "v-le-parc-l-hotel-du-parc", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 4, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU LIST", "name": "Le Parc — L'Hôtel du Parc", "short": "Le Parc — L'Hôtel du Parc", "lat": 43.186937, "lng": 6.5383919, "tags": ["Cavalaire · Le Parc", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["bistronomic hotel table"], "why": "The renovated Hôtel du Parc has 36 rooms in a 7,000 m² wooded park about 350 metres from the beach. Jean-Brice Ruffroy cooks a short southern carte from Mediterranean produce, with spices from further off, served on a large terrace over the park. Lunch moves to the pool bar from May to September, and two theme nights a week, a barbecue and a Mediterranean sharing table, change the room's pace.", "address": "Call ahead: tourist listing gives 7 avenue du Fleuriste; the hotel listing gives 8041 avenue des Orangers, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Parc+%E2%80%94+L%27H%C3%B4tel+du+Parc+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "chefs", "verdict": "In a town with no Michelin table, this is the one credible sit-down. Chef Jean-Brice Ruffroy, a veteran of starred kitchens, runs a bistronomic carte off named Provençal produce — Provence asparagus, Sisteron lamb, Perthuis potatoes — and the pêche du jour, served in the dining room or on the terrace over the park. Falsifiable: if you sit down and the menu isn't built on that named local sourcing plus a daily catch, this card is wrong.", "signature": "Label Rouge pork with artichokes and phô broth — the dish Gault&Millau flags first as the signature; the pêche du jour (daily catch) carries the fish billing.", "caveat": "The signal is a selection and a phrase, not a rating: Gault&Millau lists it with no toque and no /20 score published, and there is no Michelin nod — a resort town this size barely registers with the guides (Michelin: zero starred tables in Cavalaire). Naming is muddled too (the room reads as 'Le Parc'; the dinner service is branded 'La Table du Parc'). And note the honest mismatch: the guide-flagged marquee dishes are actually meat and fruit (Label Rouge pork, coquelet, apricot three ways) despite the fish-forward billing. It's a 4-star hotel restaurant — evening-focused, not a cheap eat — and seasonal: the dining room runs roughly late April to early October (closed Mondays), while in winter the chef and brigade decamp to Avoriaz.", "person": "Jean-Brice Ruffroy", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M selection", "full": "Listed by Gault&Millau (page © 2025, marked sponsored), no toque or /20 score; no MICHELIN listing.", "cosign": "Chef Jean-Brice Ruffroy"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Cavalaire's hotel table under the park's pines: a southern bistronomic carte, evenings only, in season.", "dishes": [{"name": "Label Rouge pork, artichokes and phô broth", "note": "The dish Gault&Millau names first", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pêche du jour", "note": "The daily catch", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Coquelet", "note": "One of the guide-flagged mains", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Apricot three ways", "note": "The dessert the guide singles out", "kind": "sweet"}], "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · Le Parc", "hours": "Seasonal: 24 Apr–3 Oct 2026, closed Monday (tourist listing updated 7 May 2026); closed now, reopens spring 2027 (date not announced)"},
    {"id": "v-la-pinede-plage", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 5, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU 12/20", "name": "La Pinède Plage", "short": "La Pinède Plage", "lat": 43.1757, "lng": 6.5877, "tags": ["La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["private-beach Mediterranean seafood"], "why": "La Pinède Plage is the beach restaurant of a four-star hotel at the eastern end of Gigaro, and it seats about 120 under the pines. Gault&Millau wrote that the menu is aimed at yachtsmen on stopover, and leads with an octopus carpaccio. The executive menu carries Thomas Pézéril's name, and part of the vegetables come from the Château de Valmer kitchen garden up the road, so the beach lunch shares a garden with the starred room.", "address": "382 Boulevard de Gigaro, 83420 La Croix-Valmer, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Pin%C3%A8de+Plage+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "sand", "verdict": "The most credible feet-near-the-sand table on Gigaro. A pine-shaded terrace facing the Îles d'Or where the octopus arrives cooked in court-bouillon, dressed as a carpaccio under a lemon-confit condiment with capers, tomato, rocket and pine nuts, alongside a tuna niçoise and shellfish linguine. Be clear about what it is: not a gastronomic temple. Gault&Millau gives it one toque and 12/20 — the mark of a very good beach kitchen, not a great restaurant. But on a shore where most tables coast on the view alone, this one actually cooks, and cooks with produce from the Château de Valmer's own kitchen garden.", "signature": "Octopus cooked in court-bouillon, served carpaccio-style with a lemon-confit condiment, capers, tomato, rocket and pine nuts (the dish Gault&Millau leads its review with); the house \"burger de la Pinède\" is the casual counterpoint on the same card.", "caveat": "This is the restaurant of a 4-star hotel's private beach: resort pricing, a yacht-stopover crowd (Gault&Millau notes it accommodates \"yachtsmen on stopover\"), and a short season — it runs only 30 April to 10 October (2026 dates confirmed), shut the rest of the year. One honest note on the signal: Gault&Millau's live page dates the 12/20 / one-toque rating to 2025, not 2026, so it is the current guide's score but carries a 2025 stamp. The executive menu is Thomas Pézéril's; the hotel's own site names Massimiliano d'Amico as the chef executing it day to day.", "person": "Thomas Pézéril", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 12/20 2025", "full": "Gault&Millau 12/20, one toque, Table Gourmande (2025 review; no 2026 review found).", "cosign": "Gault&Millau"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "A hotel's pine-shaded beach terrace on Gigaro, with one Gault&Millau toque and the Îles d'Or ahead.", "dishes": [{"name": "Octopus carpaccio", "note": "Cooked in court-bouillon, under lemon confit, capers, tomato, rocket and pine nuts", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tuna niçoise", "note": "The tuna plate built on the salade niçoise", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Shellfish linguine", "note": "The pasta of the card", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Burger de la Pinède", "note": "The casual counterpoint", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "EUR 30–120 (tourist listing, seen Oct 2026)", "web": "https://www.pinedeplage.com", "neighborhood": "La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "hours": "Seasonal: 30 Apr–10 Oct 2026 daily (Saint-Tropez Tourisme; one regional listing says to 4 Oct); closed now, reopens spring 2027 (date not announced)"},
    {"id": "v-marius-a-la-plage", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 6, "badge": "GIGARO ON THE SAND", "name": "Marius à la plage", "short": "Marius à la plage", "lat": 43.1840552, "lng": 6.5985997, "tags": ["La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Beach restaurant, pieds dans l'eau (contemporary Provençal + grilled fish)"], "why": "Arthur and Sarah spent two years running a first Le Marius at the port, then moved in April 2012 to the protected far end of Gigaro, where the coastal path climbs toward Cap Lardier. The name is Arthur's grandfather's, and so is the house dessert. The card is short and Provençal: squid and sardines off the plancha, ratatouille made in the kitchen, and in high summer a musical apéritif as the light goes.", "address": "Plage de Gigaro, 83420 La Croix-Valmer (Golfe de Saint-Tropez, near Cavalaire-sur-Mer)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Marius+%C3%A0+la+plage+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "sand", "verdict": "The tables sit literally on the sand in the preserved bay at the far end of Gigaro, feet from the turquoise. Contemporary Provençal cooking built on freshly caught, grilled Mediterranean fish; in high summer, live musical apéritifs often run around 7–9pm. Run by Arthur and Sarah since April 2012.", "signature": "Calamars grillés — grilled squid with sauce vierge and homemade ratatouille (EUR 28); the sardines grillées de Méditerranée with ratatouille (EUR 24) run the same grilled-fish line. The house dessert \"Douceur du Père Marius\" (EUR 12) nods directly to the grandfather the place is named for.", "caveat": "No Michelin or Gault&Millau listing, and no critics' review surfaced in verification — this earns its place on genuine local reputation, not a guide badge. And you pay for the sand-and-sea setting: a tartare de bar main runs about EUR 49, grilled fish around EUR 24–28. Seasonal — open roughly early April (around 6 April) to the end of September; music times shift by night, so call ahead (04 94 54 25 05).", "person": "Arthur and Sarah — founders since April 2012, after two years running \"Le Marius\" at the port; named for Arthur's grandfather Marius, and set on the Gigaro beach where Arthur and Sarah's own story began.", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Tables on the sand at the far end of Gigaro: grilled fish and ratatouille, open spring to September.", "dishes": [{"name": "Calamars grillés", "note": "Grilled squid with sauce vierge and house ratatouille (EUR 28 in the old record)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Sardines grillées de Méditerranée", "note": "With ratatouille (EUR 24 in the old record)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tartare de bar", "note": "The raw sea-bass main", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Douceur du Père Marius", "note": "The dessert named for the grandfather", "kind": "sweet"}], "hours": "Seasonal: 30 Mar or 6 Apr to 30 Sep 2026 (plageprivee.com vs regional tourist listing); closed now, reopens spring 2027 (date not announced) · Daily 09:00–24:00 in season (plageprivee.com); not checked on the own site", "phone": "+33 4 94 54 25 05", "web": "https://www.mariusalaplage.fr", "neighborhood": "La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "signal_chip": {"label": "Since 2012", "full": "Run by Arthur and Sarah on Gigaro beach since April 2012; no MICHELIN or Gault&Millau listing.", "cosign": "Arthur and Sarah, founders"}},
    {"id": "v-tamaris-beach", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 7, "badge": "GRANDE PLAGE", "name": "Tamaris Beach", "short": "Tamaris Beach", "lat": 43.1752, "lng": 6.5343, "tags": ["Cavalaire · Grande Plage", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["private-beach lunch restaurant"], "why": "Tamaris sits on the Promenade de la Mer, on the town's main beach, and works as a full beach day: breakfast, loungers and parasols, a covered terrace under a straw tonnelle, a daily special, and meat and fish off the plancha. Its season is one of the longest on the bay, from late March into autumn, so after the Gigaro tables shut it is one of the last places in Cavalaire to eat lunch with the sea a few metres away.", "address": "Promenade de la Mer, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Tamaris+Beach+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "sand", "verdict": "Come for a feet-in-the-sand lunch under the straw tonnelle on Cavalaire's main beach: a salad or a plancha-grilled local fish, water lapping a few metres away, open daily in season from 9h (closing between 18h and 20h depending on the source). It's an honest beach table, not a kitchen with ambition — arrive expecting gastronomy and you'll be let down; the fish and the sand are the whole point.", "signature": "Poisson local grille a la plancha — locally caught fish on the plancha, the anchor of a short card of salads and grilled meats and fish.", "caveat": "No Michelin or Gault&Millau listing, and none should be expected — this is a plage-privee lunch spot, not a gastronomic address. Seasonal only (published dates 30 March to 5 November); a day package of two loungers and a parasol runs about EUR 42 and a midday meal about EUR 25, so it's a beach-club sit-down, not cheap eats. No owner or chef is named in any source.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Beach table", "full": "A private-beach lunch table on Cavalaire's Grande Plage; named in Little Weekends' Cavalaire guide (undated). No MICHELIN or Gault&Millau listing.", "cosign": "Little Weekends"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "A straw-roofed beach table on Cavalaire's Grande Plage, plancha fish at lunch, open into the autumn.", "dishes": [{"name": "Poisson local à la plancha", "note": "Locally caught fish on the griddle, the anchor of the card", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Plat du jour", "note": "The daily special", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Fresh salads", "note": "The light side of a short lunch card", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Seasonal: 2026 dates disputed — 25 Mar–5 Nov, 27 Mar–30 Oct, or 30 Mar–5 Nov (tourist and beach listings); probably still open now, call ahead · Call ahead: Little Weekends gives daily 9:00–18:00 in season; beach directories give closing up to 20:00", "phone": "+33 6 63 19 31 83", "price_range": "Two loungers and a parasol about EUR 42 a day, lunch about EUR 25 (spiagge.it, undated; seen Oct 2026)", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · Grande Plage", "hot_this_month": "Last lunches on the sand: listed open until 5 November 2026, plancha fish under the straw tonnelle; phone before going (checked 21 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-le-comptoir-des-saveurs", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 9, "badge": "PORT TABLE", "name": "Le Comptoir des Saveurs", "short": "Le Comptoir des Saveurs", "lat": 43.17086, "lng": 6.535284, "tags": ["Cavalaire · Nouveau Port", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Provençal port table"], "why": "The house plays the old Provençal ritual straight: salt cod, boiled vegetables and a garlic sauce thick enough to hold a spoon, the dish Provençal villages still serve at their summer feasts. The Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist listing files it as a fish restaurant with home-made cooking, right on the port, so the boats you watch from the terrace are the same fleet that sells its catch a few hundred metres along the quay.", "address": "Nouveau Port, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer, Var, Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Comptoir+des+Saveurs+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "fish", "verdict": "Order the traditional aïoli (aggregated listings put the formula around €29.50): salt cod, boiled seasonal vegetables and a thick, garlic-forward sauce — the real Provençal ritual, not a decorative version. Get that plate right, plus a pissaladière and the red-wine daube of beef cheek, and this is honest port cooking. If the aïoli comes garlic-shy and under-seasoned, or the pissaladière tastes sweet and jarred, this card is wrong.", "signature": "Aïoli provençal complet (~€29.50, per aggregated listings) — salt cod and boiled vegetables under a garlic-heavy sauce; the daube de joue de bœuf braised in red wine and the pissaladière are the supporting emblems the kitchen leans on, all three named on the restaurant's own site and the regional tourism board.", "caveat": "No guide backs this; it rests on local standing and review sites. The tourist listing gives a March–October season, the old record said February–October: in late October call before walking down. Closed days per the house site (Wednesday off, dinner only Monday, Tuesday and Thursday) were not rechecked. Aggregated reviews include complaints about fish served raw and about service; the aïoli formula is the value play.", "person": "Cécile Paolini — listed as the owner/responsible party (SARL EMIPE, SIRET 500567516) on the restaurant's own website. It reads as owner-run; whether she also cooks the line is not confirmed, so treat her as the proprietor, not verified as chef.", "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Owner-run fish and Provençal table on Cavalaire's Nouveau Port, built around the grand aïoli.", "dishes": [{"name": "Aïoli provençal complet", "note": "Salt cod, boiled seasonal vegetables and a garlic-forward aïoli; the value formula on the card", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Daube de joue de bœuf", "note": "Beef cheek braised in red wine, the house's slow Provençal plate", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pissaladière", "note": "Onion, black olive and anchovy tart, a Niçois classic served as a starter", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Moules", "note": "The mussels most often praised in aggregated reviews", "kind": "dish"}], "phone": "+33 4 94 05 46 10", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · Nouveau Port", "signal_chip": {"label": "Port table", "full": "Owner-run Provençal and fish restaurant on the Nouveau Port, listed by the Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist office; no Michelin or Gault&Millau entry", "cosign": "Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist listing (seen Oct 2026)"}, "hours": "Seasonal: March to October per the Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist listing (2026 opening days not shown); status at 21 Oct not confirmed: call ahead, closing for the winter is due around now"},
    {"id": "v-le-grand-bleu-hotel-de-la-cala", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 10, "badge": "CLIFF TERRACE", "name": "Le Grand Bleu — Hôtel de la Calanque", "short": "Le Grand Bleu — Hôtel de la Calanque", "lat": 43.1686, "lng": 6.5451, "tags": ["Cavalaire · La Calanque", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Cliffside seafood terrace"], "why": "The Hôtel de la Calanque stands on the rocks above the Cron cove, and its restaurant terrace hangs over the cliff edge, done in white and blue in the Capri manner. It is one of two restaurants in the hotel, which the Cavalaire tourist office lists as open daily from 25 March to 6 November 2026, so it is one of the last terraces on the bay still serving dinner when the port tables have shut.", "address": "20 rue (or avenue) de la Calanque, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Grand+Bleu+%E2%80%94+H%C3%B4tel+de+la+Calanque+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "fish", "verdict": "A view-first fish house on a terrace pinned to the cliff above the Cron cove, east of Cavalaire's port: come for the drop to the sea and the day's fish. What it is not: a critics' address. No MICHELIN listing; Gault&Millau carries a directory entry without a toque or a score (seen Jul 2026). It is a member of the chef-founded Collège Culinaire de France (since 2024) — peer recognition, not a critic's ranking.", "signature": "The bouillabaisse de la calanque with its aïoli and a lobster risotto were the house plates in July 2026; neither shows on the 2026 menus seen in October, where reviews point to the cuttlefish linguine — ask what is on.", "caveat": "Hours drift by source and season — this is a seasonal Riviera hotel-restaurant, so confirm it's open before you go. Little Weekends lists dinner daily plus weekend lunches; the hotel's own page lists lunch daily (12:00–17:00) and dinner Tuesday–Sunday (19:00–21:30); Gault&Millau shows yet another weekly pattern. The reputation is built on the setting first: per Little Weekends, entrées 18–24€, plats 28–36€, desserts 15€. Come for the terrace and the calanque, not for a critical pedigree that doesn't exist.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Cliff terrace", "full": "Hotel restaurant on the cliff above the Cron cove; Gault&Millau directory entry without a score; member of the Collège Culinaire de France since 2024", "cosign": "Little Weekends city guide; Cavalaire tourist office listing 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Hotel restaurant on a terrace over the Cron cove, east of Cavalaire port, open until early November 2026.", "dishes": [{"name": "Bouillabaisse de la calanque", "note": "Served with its aïoli; the house dish in the old record, not re-confirmed on a 2026 menu", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Risotto au homard", "note": "Lobster risotto, the other plate the old record names", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Linguine with cuttlefish and parsley", "note": "The dish recent review extracts single out most", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Seasonal: hotel open daily 25 March to 6 November 2026 (Cavalaire tourist office listing, updated 9 June 2026); open now, closes 6 Nov; 2027 reopening not announced. A booking site also shows a closure from 20 September 2026 (dates 'not definitive'): call ahead · Call ahead: review aggregators give dinner daily 19:00–22:30 with lunch Saturday and Sunday 12:00–14:30; the hotel's own page (seen July 2026) gave lunch daily 12:00–17:00 and dinner Tuesday–Sunday 19:00–21:30", "phone": "+33 4 94 01 95 00", "web": "https://www.hoteldelacalanque.com", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · La Calanque", "charter": {"warn": "Hours drift by source and season — this is a seasonal Riviera hotel-restaurant, so confirm it's open before you go. Little Weekends lists dinner daily plus weekend lunches; the hotel's own page lists lunch daily (12:00–17:00) and dinner Tuesday–Sunday (19:00–21:30); Gault&Millau shows yet another weekly pattern. The reputation is built on the setting first: per Little Weekends, entrées 18–24€, plats 28–36€, desserts 15€. Come for the terrace and the calanque, not for a critical pedigree that doesn't exist.", "fit": "Hotel restaurant on a terrace over the Cron cove, east of Cavalaire port, open until early November 2026."}},
    {"id": "v-le-refuge", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 11, "badge": "GIGARO TERRACE", "name": "Le Refuge", "short": "Le Refuge", "lat": 43.1878, "lng": 6.579, "tags": ["La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Seaside seasonal table"], "why": "Le Refuge opened under Gigaro's parasol pines in 1964 and came back in 2019 after a long rebuild, with a raised terrace of about 180 seats looking over the bay towards Cap Lardier and candles on the tables at night. The tourist listings say the kitchen buys from local growers and the small boats of the coast, and in autumn 2026 it still serves lunch and dinner five days a week, to the end of October.", "address": "1412 Boulevard de Gigaro, 83420 La Croix-Valmer (Gigaro), near Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Refuge+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "fish", "verdict": "Come for a produce-led lunch on the broad sea-view terrace over Gigaro bay: the card reads to the season and leans on the local catch and Mediterranean produce, not a printed year-round list. If it turns up as a fixed tourist menu of frozen fish, the whole reason to choose it over the neighbouring beach spots is gone.", "signature": "No single fixed signature — the card changes week to week with the season. Cooking leans on local produce and, per the house, \"traditional fishermen\"; the standing seafood option is The Fisherman's Plate (assiette du pecheur, ~38 euros), set alongside land plates like confit Sisteron lamb shoulder and a roasted Bresse-style poultry dish, so the sea is only ever part of the story.", "caveat": "No Michelin or Gault&Millau ranking, and no press feature surfaced — it earns its place on local standing and a seasonal, produce-led kitchen, not a guide score. It is seaside, not a working quay: a terrace among Gigaro's centenary parasol pines overlooking the bay (Petit Fute lists it as a \"restaurant de plage\"). Seasonal operation — the 2026 season ran roughly 7 Mar to 31 Oct — though the house site confusingly also says \"open all year.\" Chef attribution is unsettled: the current site credits Laurent Latreille while 2025 tourism listings still name Sebastien Massart (weekly-changing menu), so a recent handover is likely; neither is independently confirmed, so no chef is asserted here. Founded 1964, reopened 2019 after a long renovation. Coordinates are approximate.", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Pine-shaded terrace over Gigaro bay, cooking from local fishermen and farms, open until 31 October 2026.", "dishes": [{"name": "Assiette du pêcheur", "note": "The standing fisherman's plate of the day's catch (about EUR 38 in the old record)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Confit Sisteron lamb shoulder", "note": "The land plate the old record names beside the fish", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Tapas on the terrace", "note": "Small plates the tourist listings name as a house speciality", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Seasonal: 7 March to 31 October 2026; open now, closes 31 Oct; 2027 reopening not announced · 14 Sept–31 Oct 2026: Monday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday and Sunday 12:00–14:30 and 19:00–21:30; closed Tuesday and Wednesday (La Croix-Valmer tourist listing, updated March 2026)", "price_range": "EUR 30–60 or 40–80 depending on the listing (seen Oct 2026)", "web": "https://www.lerefugegigaro.fr", "neighborhood": "La Croix-Valmer · Gigaro", "signal_chip": {"label": "Gigaro terrace", "full": "Seaside restaurant under the pines of Gigaro, founded 1964, reopened 2019; no Michelin or Gault&Millau ranking", "cosign": "La Croix-Valmer tourist office listings, 2026"}, "hot_this_month": "On its autumn timetable until 31 October 2026: lunch and dinner Monday, Thursday, Friday and weekends, closed Tuesday and Wednesday (checked 21 Oct 2026).", "charter": {"price": "EUR 30–60 or 40–80 depending on the listing (seen Oct 2026)", "warn": "No Michelin or Gault&Millau ranking, and no press feature surfaced — it earns its place on local standing and a seasonal, produce-led kitchen, not a guide score. It is seaside, not a working quay: a terrace among Gigaro's centenary parasol pines overlooking the bay (Petit Fute lists it as a \"restaurant de plage\"). Seasonal operation — the 2026 season ran roughly 7 Mar to 31 Oct — though the house site confusingly also says \"open all year.\" Chef attribution is unsettled: the current site credits Laurent Latreille while 2025 tourism listings still name Sebastien Massart (weekly-changing menu), so a recent handover is likely; neither is independently confirmed, so no chef is asserted here. Founded 1964, reopened 2019 after a long renovation. Coordinates are approximate.", "fit": "Pine-shaded terrace over Gigaro bay, cooking from local fishermen and farms, open until 31 October 2026."}},
    {"id": "v-maurin-des-maures", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 2, "badge": "PUDLOWSKI 2025", "name": "Maurin des Maures", "short": "Maurin des Maures", "lat": 43.158, "lng": 6.473, "tags": ["Le Rayol-Canadel · village", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Provençal bistrot / coast institution"], "why": "The room began as a 1920s bar-tabac for the men building the coast road, and André Delmonte, Dédé, has run it since 1985. Pudlowski went back in September 2025 and found it open every day from early morning, the weekday lunch menu still at EUR 22.50. The windows face the Bay of Rayol and the isles of Levant and Port-Cros, and a jazz quartet played there every Tuesday in summer 2026.", "address": "29 avenue Étienne Gola, 83820 Le Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Maurin+des+Maures+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "corniche", "verdict": "The bouillabaisse Pudlowski calls a monument: a two-person cauldron of chapon, congre, saint-pierre, grondin, vive, crabe and moules that you must phone in the day before. Skip that call and there's little reason to make the drive down to the cove.", "signature": "Bouillabaisse du pêcheur, served for at least two people, à commander la veille (chapon, congre, saint-pierre, grondin, vive, crabe, moules, safran, huile d'olive) — €76.50 for a pot serving two, per Pudlowski, 2022.", "caveat": "Order the bouillabaisse the day before — Pudlowski's exact phrase is \"à commander la veille\", not the \"48h\" sometimes quoted. Treat €76.50 for a pot serving two as Pudlowski's 2022 figure: the current menu lists the bouillabaisse du pêcheur at around €85.50 a person, two people minimum (seen Jul 2026). A splurge plate, not a cheap eat. No current Guide du Routard listing was found: the signal rests on Pudlowski and Gault&Millau (11/20). One listing says no dinner from 11 November to 20 December: call.", "person": "André \"Dédé\" Del Monte — the white-moustached patron since 1985, still the host four decades on.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Pudlowski 2025", "full": "Gilles Pudlowski, 'Rayol-Canadel : Dédé l'éternel', Les Pieds dans le Plat (Sept 2025), after 'Au bonheur de Dédé' (July 2022); Gault&Millau 11/20 in the old record", "cosign": "Pudlowski calls the bouillabaisse 'à la fois un monument, un plat de mémoire et une légende.'"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Year-round bar-restaurant on the Rayol corniche, where Dédé Delmonte has served bouillabaisse since 1985.", "dishes": [{"name": "Bouillabaisse du pêcheur", "note": "Minimum two people, ordered the day before: chapon, congre, saint-pierre, grondin, vive, crab and mussels", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Terrine de bouillabaisse", "note": "A cold starter version of the soup listed on the 2026 card", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Menu du jour", "note": "The weekday lunch menu at EUR 22.50 (seen Oct 2026)", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Year-round 2026, open daily (tourist listing updated Feb 2026); one listing says dinner is not served 11 November–20 December · Call ahead: the 2026 tourist listing gives lunch to 14:00 and dinner to 21:00; Pudlowski (Sept 2025) gives 06:30–23:00 every day", "phone": "+33 4 94 05 60 11", "web": "https://www.maurin-des-maures.com", "price_range": "Menu du jour EUR 22.50; à la carte EUR 55–75 (Pudlowski, Sept 2025); bouillabaisse about EUR 85.50 per person, minimum two (seen 2026)", "reservation": "Phone the day before for the bouillabaisse", "neighborhood": "Le Rayol-Canadel · village", "charter": {"price": "Menu du jour EUR 22.50; à la carte EUR 55–75 (Pudlowski, Sept 2025); bouillabaisse about EUR 85.50 per person, minimum two (seen 2026)", "book": "Phone the day before for the bouillabaisse", "warn": "Order the bouillabaisse the day before — Pudlowski's exact phrase is \"à commander la veille\", not the \"48h\" sometimes quoted. Treat €76.50 for a pot serving two as Pudlowski's 2022 figure: the current menu lists the bouillabaisse du pêcheur at around €85.50 a person, two people minimum (seen Jul 2026). A splurge plate, not a cheap eat. No current Guide du Routard listing was found: the signal rests on Pudlowski and Gault&Millau (11/20). One listing says no dinner from 11 November to 20 December: call.", "fit": "Year-round bar-restaurant on the Rayol corniche, where Dédé Delmonte has served bouillabaisse since 1985."}},
    {"id": "v-le-cafe-des-jardiniers-domaine", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 12, "badge": "GARDEN LUNCH", "name": "Le Café des Jardiniers — Domaine du Rayol", "short": "Le Café des Jardiniers — Domaine du Rayol", "lat": 43.1554, "lng": 6.4806, "tags": ["Le Rayol-Canadel · Domaine du Rayol", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Garden café / botanical-garden lunch"], "why": "Tom Ortin, the chef, came here from the Park Hyatt Vendôme in Paris, where he cooked beside Jean-François Rouquette. At the Domaine du Rayol he works from the garden and from local growers, with a lot of vegetables on the plate, and everything including the desserts is made on site. In November 2025 the winter card opened with a velouté of winter vegetables, and in mid-October 2026 the Domaine ran a weekend of wine paired with the food.", "address": "Domaine du Rayol – Le Jardin des Méditerranées, Avenue Jacques Chirac (ex-Avenue des Belges), 83820 Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer, Var", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Caf%C3%A9+des+Jardiniers+%E2%80%94+Domaine+du+Rayol+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "corniche", "verdict": "The one lunch on this coast you reach by walking through a botanical garden of the world's Mediterranean climates. On a terrace in the heart of the Domaine du Rayol, chef Tom Ortin cooks a short, seasonal Provençal chalkboard — everything made on-site, a vegetarian plate every day, homemade ice creams. Falsifiable and verified: it exists only for people who have paid garden admission, takes no reservations (except groups of 15+), and the kitchen serves lunch only, 12:00–14:30, even though the café/tea-room runs from about 10:30 until an hour before the garden closes. Open year-round, seven days, except 25 December.", "signature": "Souris d'agneau au jus de thym du jardin — lamb shank in a jus of thyme cut from the garden, with grenaille potatoes and roasted vegetables. Directly attested as an official menu example, but it is part of a daily-rotating chalkboard, so not guaranteed on any given day.", "caveat": "This is a garden-and-a-plate outing, not a restaurant you can just book. You cannot eat here without buying a Domaine du Rayol garden ticket, and it seats garden visitors only — the ticket does not guarantee a table. No reservations except groups of 15+; at busy times it's a wait-list on site. The kitchen is lunch-only (12:00–14:30); outside that the terrace serves drinks, a gourmet break and ice cream, not full plates. The \"nougat\" ice cream flavour and the \"Little Weekends\" guide mention could not be verified and have been dropped — homemade/artisanal ice creams are confirmed, but the specific flavour rotates.", "person": "Chef Tom Ortin", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Lunch-only garden café inside the Domaine du Rayol, open all year to garden visitors.", "dishes": [{"name": "Souris d'agneau au jus de thym du jardin", "note": "Lamb shank in a jus of thyme from the garden; an official example from the rotating board", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Winter vegetable velouté", "note": "The soup Ortin's team put on in November 2025", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Daily vegetarian plate", "note": "A meat-free plate every day, from local and garden produce", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Homemade ice creams", "note": "Made on site; flavours rotate", "kind": "sweet"}], "hours": "Year-round, every day except 25 December · Winter (Nov–Mar, per the Domaine's site): café 10:30–16:30, lunch 12:00–14:30; garden 9:30–17:30. Autumn hours for late October not seen", "phone": "+33 4 98 04 44 00", "web": "https://www.domainedurayol.org", "neighborhood": "Le Rayol-Canadel · Domaine du Rayol", "signal_chip": {"label": "Garden lunch", "full": "Café-restaurant inside the Conservatoire du littoral's Domaine du Rayol, garden by Gilles Clément; chef Tom Ortin; covered by Pudlowski (Sept 2025)", "cosign": "Domaine du Rayol site; Pudlowski, Les Pieds dans le Plat, Sept 2025"}, "hot_this_month": "The Domaine du Rayol garden is open every day on winter hours; a gardener-led walk runs on 20 November 2026; check the café is serving lunch before you plan on it (checked 21 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-bello-visto", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 16, "badge": "MICHELIN SELECTED", "name": "Bello Visto", "short": "Bello Visto", "neighborhood": "Gassin · Place dei Barri", "tags": ["MICHELIN selection, ramparts terrace", "Gassin"], "productTags": ["bib-gourmand", "provencal", "sea-view"], "why": "MICHELIN describes the chef as a veteran and a local lad whose grandmother lived in the village, and the house runs on the Barri, the old walk around Gassin's walls, with seven rooms and a village bar alongside. The card leans on the coast and the hill: octopus from the rocks stewed in a casserole, gnocchi with truffle, and a Grand Marnier soufflé to finish, with the Gulf of Saint-Tropez spread below the terrace.", "address": "Place deis Barri, old village, 83580 Gassin", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bello+Visto+Cavalaire-sur-Mer+Var", "category": "villages", "verdict": "Come for traditional Provençal cooking with the gulf laid out below the ramparts: the octopus casserole, the truffle gnocchi and the Grand Marnier soufflé are the plates MICHELIN names. Falsifiable claim: it is in the MICHELIN selection as a €€€ traditional table and Gault&Millau scores it 13/20, Table de Chef, about EUR 43–65 a head; it is not a Bib Gourmand, whatever older lists say.", "signature": "The seasonal Provencal mains and the day's fish and octopus preparations; ask for the day's suggestion and sit on the rampart terrace at sunset, not inside.", "person": "Sylvain Humbert", "caveat": "Booking: the famous view means the terrace tables are fought over, so reserve and request outside. It is a small village hotel-restaurant, not a scene, which is exactly the appeal.", "hook": "Village hotel-restaurant on Gassin's rampart, in the MICHELIN selection; its 2026 season ended 11 October.", "signal_chip": {"label": "MICHELIN", "full": "In the MICHELIN Guide selection (€€€, traditional cuisine; no Bib Gourmand shown); Gault&Millau 13/20, Table de Chef", "cosign": "Chef Sylvain Humbert; Laurence Humbert front of house"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "dishes": [{"name": "Cassolette de poulpe de roche", "note": "Rock octopus casserole, a house speciality on the MICHELIN page", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Gnocchi à la truffe", "note": "Truffle gnocchi, also named by MICHELIN", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Soufflé au Grand Marnier", "note": "The dessert MICHELIN lists among the specialities", "kind": "sweet"}, {"name": "Suggestion du jour", "note": "The day's fish from the coast; ask for it", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "EUR 43–65 per person before drinks (Gault&Millau); MICHELIN price band €€€ (seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book and ask for the rampart terrace", "subcat": "MICHELIN selection, ramparts terrace", "hours": "Seasonal: 24 April–11 October 2026 (tourist listing); closed now, reopens spring 2027 (date not announced)"},
    {"id": "v-la-verdoyante", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 17, "badge": "FAMILY TABLE", "name": "La Verdoyante", "short": "La Verdoyante", "neighborhood": "Gassin · Coste Brigade vineyards", "tags": ["Gassin · Coste Brigade vineyards", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["provencal", "garrigue", "vineyard-view"], "why": "Two generations of the Mouret family have run this farmhouse table on the slope below Gassin, and the wide terrace faces the Minuty vineyard with the Gulf of Saint-Tropez beyond. It takes bookings by telephone only, and from June to early October the tourist office warns it is full several days ahead. The cooking is southern home food scented with the hill's thyme and rosemary, fish soup and bourride among the classics.", "address": "866 chemin vicinal de Coste-Brigade, 83580 Gassin", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Verdoyante+Cavalaire-sur-Mer+Var", "category": "villages", "verdict": "The anti-Saint-Tropez table: no scene, no DJ, just southern home cooking on a wide terrace over the vines of Minuty, run by two generations of the Mouret family. Falsifiable claim: it is listed by Gault&Millau, Le Routard and Petit Futé and books out days ahead in high season; a MICHELIN listing could not be confirmed for 2026.", "signature": "Whatever is built on the day's garden and herbs, the kitchen leans hard into thyme-and-rosemary garrigue flavours, plus classics like fish soup and bourride; the panoramic terrace over the domaines is the reason to book lunch.", "person": "", "caveat": "Seasonal (roughly spring to autumn) and it is out among the vineyards, so you need a car. Honesty by negation: comfort and setting over cutting-edge cooking.", "hook": "Mouret family table in the Gassin vineyards, terrace facing Minuty and the sea; 2026 season ended 4 October.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Family table", "full": "Mouret family restaurant over the Gassin vineyards; listed by Gault&Millau, Le Routard and Petit Futé (tourist office); MICHELIN 2026 not confirmed", "cosign": "Saint-Tropez Tourisme listing, 2026 season"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "dishes": [{"name": "Soupe de poissons", "note": "The house fish soup, one of the classics in the record", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Bourride", "note": "White fish in a garlic aïoli-thickened broth, the Provençal cousin of bouillabaisse", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Garrigue-herb plates", "note": "The day's dishes built on thyme and rosemary from the hills", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Seasonal: 29 April–4 October 2026 (Saint-Tropez Tourisme); closed now, reopens spring 2027 (date not announced) · 2026 season: closed Monday; Tuesday and Friday lunch only (9:30–15:00); Wednesday, Thursday and weekends 9:30–15:00 and 18:00–23:00 (Saint-Tropez Tourisme)", "phone": "+33 4 94 56 16 23", "web": "https://www.la-verdoyante.fr", "reservation": "Telephone only; full several days ahead from June to early October"},
    {"id": "v-la-forge", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 18, "badge": "GAULT&MILLAU 13", "name": "La Forge", "short": "La Forge", "neighborhood": "Ramatuelle · village", "tags": ["Ramatuelle · village", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["italian", "fresh-pasta", "village"], "why": "Paolo Amadori comes from Le Marche and cooks with Italian produce in what was the village forge; pasta and bread are made in the house and the card follows the season. Gault&Millau scores it 13/20 and names the vitello tonnato and the osso buco. It keeps the longest season of the hill villages' tables in 2026, every day from 2 April to 1 November, so it is one of the few still lit in Ramatuelle this week.", "address": "11 rue Victor Léon, 83350 Ramatuelle", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Forge+Cavalaire-sur-Mer+Var", "category": "villages", "verdict": "Ramatuelle village's most convincing non-French table: a chef from Le Marche and a Piedmont-Lake-Maggiore host who cook Italy straight, from a panoramic terrace in an old forge. Falsifiable claim: it is run by chef Paolo Amadori and Francesca Alessi, both Italian-born, not a French 'trattoria' concept.", "signature": "Fresh house-made pasta and bread and the day's Italian fish; the kitchen cooks regional Italy, so order whatever is most Italian and least generic Cote d'Azur.", "person": "Paolo Amadori", "caveat": "Price and booking: this is village-near-Saint-Tropez pricing and summer-busy, and the good terrace tables need reserving. Honest cooking, not a bargain.", "hook": "Italian-run trattoria in Ramatuelle's old forge, house pasta and bread, open daily until 1 November 2026.", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 13/20", "full": "Gault&Millau 13/20 (review labelled 2025); earlier praise from Gilles Pudlowski and Jacques Gantié", "cosign": "Chef Paolo Amadori (Le Marche); Francesca Alessi front of house (2017 source)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "dishes": [{"name": "Fresh house pasta", "note": "Made in the kitchen daily; the reason to come", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Vitello tonnato", "note": "Cold veal in tuna sauce, named in the Gault&Millau review", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Osso buco", "note": "Braised veal shank, also named by Gault&Millau", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "House bread", "note": "Baked on site", "kind": "dish"}], "price_range": "À la carte EUR 50–80 (tourist office); Gault&Millau about EUR 39 a head before drinks (seen Oct 2026)", "hours": "Seasonal: daily 2 April–1 November 2026 (Ramatuelle tourist office); open now, closes 1 Nov", "charter": {"price": "À la carte EUR 50–80 (tourist office); Gault&Millau about EUR 39 a head before drinks (seen Oct 2026)", "warn": "Price and booking: this is village-near-Saint-Tropez pricing and summer-busy, and the good terrace tables need reserving. Honest cooking, not a bargain.", "fit": "Italian-run trattoria in Ramatuelle's old forge, house pasta and bread, open daily until 1 November 2026."}},
    {"id": "v-pecheurs-du-port", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 1, "name": "The fishermen's stand, Quai Marc Pajot", "short": "The fishermen's stand", "lat": null, "lng": null, "tags": ["Cavalaire · Vieux Port, Quai Marc Pajot", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Direct sale off the boats"], "address": "Quai Marc Pajot, Port de Cavalaire, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer (by the fishermen's auction point)", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Quai+Marc+Pajot+Cavalaire-sur-Mer+Var+France", "why": "Cavalaire's small boats still sell part of their catch themselves, on the quay where they tie up, rather than sending all of it to auction. The town's own page says the meeting time follows the boats and the weather, from about ten, and that the fishermen hand out recipes with the fish: bream, red mullet, girelles, rockfish for the soup, a langouste on a good day. The coast's fishermen have judged their own sea since the Middle Ages; the fold at the top tells it.", "verdict": "Cavalaire keeps a small working fleet, and the boats sell straight off the quay most mornings from about 10:00, catch and weather permitting: girelle, rascasse and chapon for the soupe de roche, sea bream and sea bass, sometimes a spider crab or a langouste.", "signature": "Rockfish for soup; the day's bream, bass and red mullet.", "caveat": "Weather and catch decide: some mornings there is nothing, and in winter the boats go out less. The town does not name the fishermen or say which days of the year the stall runs. Bring cash and a cool bag; payment methods not confirmed.", "person": "", "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Cavalaire's fishermen sell the morning's catch on Quai Marc Pajot from about 10:00, weather allowing.", "dishes": [{"name": "Rockfish for soupe de roche", "note": "Girelle, rascasse and small fish too small to sell whole, the base of the local soup", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Sea bream and sea bass", "note": "The day's main fish, named by the town", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Red mullet", "note": "Rouget, named in the town's list", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Langouste or spider crab", "note": "On some days only, catch permitting", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Every morning per the town and regional tourism pages; whether it runs through the winter is not stated · From about 10:00, depending on the boats' return and the weather (Cavalaire town site); the old guide said roughly 10:00–12:00", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · Vieux Port, Quai Marc Pajot", "badge": "OFF THE BOAT", "signal_chip": {"label": "Off the boat", "full": "Direct sale by Cavalaire's fishermen on Quai Marc Pajot, listed by the town and the regional tourism board", "cosign": "Cavalaire-sur-Mer town site; Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur tourisme"}, "category": "street", "hot_this_month": "Autumn mornings on the quay from about 10:00, weather and catch permitting: rockfish for the soupe de roche, the day's bream, bass and red mullet. Whether the stand works every winter morning is not confirmed: go and look."},
    {"id": "v-marche-place-jean-moulin", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 3, "name": "Wednesday market, Place Jean Moulin", "short": "Wednesday market", "lat": null, "lng": null, "tags": ["Cavalaire · centre, Place Jean Moulin", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 21 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Provençal market"], "address": "Place Jean Moulin, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Place+Jean+Moulin+Cavalaire-sur-Mer+Var+France", "why": "Every Wednesday of the year the stalls go up on Place Jean Moulin, a few streets back from the port: olives, tapenade and anchoïade, pissaladière and pan bagnat to eat on the way, honey from the Maures, and the rosé of the Golfe de Saint-Tropez. In winter it is the town's own market, after the summer crowd has gone, and the one morning to stock a galley with Provençal pantry food without driving to Saint-Tropez.", "verdict": "Around a hundred stalls every Wednesday morning, year-round, roughly 08:30–13:00: tapenade and anchoïade, olives, honey and olive oil, cheese and charcuterie, fish and seasonal fruit, and the rosé of the gulf.", "signature": "Tapenade, anchoïade, honey of the Maures, the gulf's rosé.", "caveat": "Morning only: it folds by one. Clothing, jewellery and craft stalls share the square with the food. The 'around a hundred stalls' count in the old guide was not confirmed.", "person": "", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Cavalaire's year-round Provençal market, every Wednesday morning on Place Jean Moulin, 08:30–13:00.", "dishes": [{"name": "Tapenade and anchoïade", "note": "Olive and anchovy spreads, the market's staple", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pissaladière", "note": "Onion and anchovy tart sold by the slice", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Pan bagnat", "note": "The Niçois sandwich of tuna, egg and raw vegetables", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Maures honey", "note": "Rosemary, lavender, thyme or chestnut, from a producer on the market", "kind": "sweet"}, {"name": "Golfe de Saint-Tropez rosé", "note": "Côtes de Provence from the nearby estates", "kind": "drink"}], "hours": "Year-round, every Wednesday (Le Lavandou tourist office, 2026 dates) · Wednesday 08:30–13:00", "phone": "+33 4 94 01 92 10 (Cavalaire tourist office)", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · centre, Place Jean Moulin", "badge": "WEDNESDAY MARKET", "signal_chip": {"label": "Wed market", "full": "Provençal market, Place Jean Moulin, every Wednesday 08:30–13:00, all year", "cosign": "Saint-Tropez Tourisme; Le Lavandou tourist office; Cavalaire town site"}, "category": "street", "hot_this_month": "The Wednesday morning market carries on all winter; 11 November, Armistice Day, falls on a Wednesday, so check it is held that week (checked 21 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-o-petit-bar", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 14, "badge": "PORT APERO BAR", "name": "Ô Petit Bar", "short": "Ô Petit Bar", "lat": 43.1729, "lng": 6.5338, "tags": ["Cavalaire · Port", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["Port apéro & cocktail bar"], "why": "This is where the port has its drink: a narrow bar in the Résidence du Port with the marina in front of it, breakfast and brunch in the morning, then cocktails, wine and local beer from the late afternoon. The tourist listing describes it as open every day of the year, which, if it holds, makes it one of the few places on the quay still pouring when the seasonal terraces have stacked their chairs.", "address": "24 Résidence du Port, Rue du Port, 83240 Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=%C3%94+Petit+Bar+Cavalaire-sur-Mer", "category": "street", "verdict": "Come for the drink, not the meal. This is a small, convivial port bar with a clear view over the Cavalaire marina where the pour — a mojito — is the point; the kitchen never leaves snack territory (roasted-cheese tartines, croque-style toasts, house pesto and tapenade). Falsifiable: turn up on an open evening and you'll find it pouring cocktails toward 1am with the harbour in front of you, not a plated dinner service — anyone arriving hungry for a proper meal will be disappointed.", "signature": "The mojito — the house drink the travel blog Little Weekends calls \"les meilleurs mojitos de la région.\" Everything else stays deliberately light: cocktails with or without alcohol, Îles d'Or craft beers, organic juices, and roasted-cheese/croque-style tartines.", "caveat": "Not a dining recommendation and carries no authority ranking — no Michelin, Gault&Millau, Routard or 50 Best entry exists for a port bar this small; the only cosign is a travel blog's \"best mojitos\" line, resting otherwise on genuine local port reputation, so treat \"best in the region\" as an enthusiast's pick, not a verdict. Hours are genuinely inconsistent across listings: Little Weekends says 18h–1h daily; the bar's own eatbu page says 17h–1h and closed Thursdays; other directories show a Wednesday closure, a ~00h30 close and a Sunday daytime-only window. Call ahead (+33 6 98 30 14 60) rather than trust one schedule, and don't count on the same closed day.", "person": "Elodie Flavigny — listed as the bar's responsable on its own online (eatbu) listing; role beyond that not independently confirmed.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Port apéro", "full": "Harbour cocktail bar; Little Weekends singles out its mojitos ('les meilleurs mojitos de la région'); no guide ranking", "cosign": "Little Weekends, Cavalaire city guide; Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist listing"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-21", "hook": "Small harbour bar on Rue du Port for the evening mojito, listed as open daily all year.", "dishes": [{"name": "Mojito", "note": "The house cocktail Little Weekends calls the best in the region", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Îles d'Or craft beer", "note": "Local beer from the islands' brewery", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Toasted cheese tartines", "note": "Croque-style snacks; the kitchen stays at bar food", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Year-round, daily, per the Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist listing (undated); not confirmed for this winter · Call ahead: Little Weekends says 18:00–01:00 daily; the bar's eatbu page says 17:00–01:00, closed Thursday; other directories show a Wednesday closure or 00:30 close", "phone": "+33 6 98 30 14 60", "neighborhood": "Cavalaire · Port"}
